# wq#2166 — start the low-power applier when an amdgpu card appears, not when
# multi-user.target is reached.
#
# hathor's boot of 2026-08-31 reached multi-user.target at monotonic 139.3s, 119s
# after the second D300 enumerated at 20.7s, so the unmatched-glob bug stayed hidden.
# On 2026-09-19 (kernel 7.0.0-31) multi-user.target came at 17.8s and the cards at
# 18.2s / 19.2s: the unit lost the race by 0.4s and 1.3s. Nothing about the ordering
# ever guaranteed the cards existed — the old boot was just slow.
#
# TAG+="systemd" makes systemd track the card; SYSTEMD_WANTS then pulls in the
# per-card instance, which is ordered After= that device.
#
# wq#2178 (found on hathor before first apply):
#   * DEVTYPE=="drm_minor" is required. KERNEL=="card[0-9]*" also matches the connector
#     nodes (card1-DP-1 .. card2-DP-12 on a D300 pair, DEVTYPE=drm_connector), and
#     DRIVERS=="amdgpu" still matches them through the parent walk. Without this every
#     boot starts one instance per connector, none of which can find the dpm knob.
#   * SYSTEMD_ALIAS is required. /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/99-systemd.rules only creates
#     sys-subsystem-*-devices-* aliases for net and bluetooth, so the
#     sys-subsystem-drm-devices-%i.device that gpu-lowpower@.service BindsTo= never
#     existed; each instance would wait out the device job timeout and fail.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="drm", KERNEL=="card[0-9]*", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="drm_minor", DRIVERS=="amdgpu", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_ALIAS}+="/sys/subsystem/drm/devices/%k", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="gpu-lowpower@%k.service"
